ctors": ["R+7 open seat gives Republicans the structural edge despite Amodei's retirement", "Flippo holds a wide fundraising and outside-spending advantage", "Strongly Democratic Nevada environment with thin polls showing a close race", "GOP rift: Amodei refusing to endorse Flippo amid an investigation request"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean and Flippo's large money advantage outweigh a favorable Nevada environment for Democrats and early polling that shows a close race; raters mostly call it Likely R and markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-four chance.", "watch": "A surge of outside spending for Benitez-Thompson or a deepening Amodei-Flippo rift could pull the race toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mark Amodei (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Mark Amodei's retirement opened Nevada's most Republican House district, and the contest has grown more competitive than usual. David Flippo, the Trump-backed Republican who won a contentious primary, has raised more than $2.2 million and benefits from over $600,000 in outside support, while Democrat Teresa Benitez-Thompson has raised about $257,000 with almost no outside help. A rift with Amodei, who has refused to endorse Flippo, and a voter request for an investigation into the Republican have given Democrats an opening.\n\nPolling is sparse and mixed: a Democratic-sponsored survey showed a one-point race, while other polls put the Republican ahead by two to six points. Raters mostly call the district Likely Republican, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-four chance. A strongly Democratic-leaning Nevada environment keeps this competitive, but the district's partisan lean and the spending gap favor Flippo.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.213, "margin": -4.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2511, "margin": -4.5}]}